{"text": " You wake up in 1915 on a prison farm in Mississippi, 11 men are lining up for breakfast. Did you know that doctors made healthy men sick here, on purpose by feeding them a poor southern family's ordinary diet? The volunteers were promised a pardon. Here's the plate, cornbread and grits, fatty salt pork, molasses, hardly any milk, fresh meat or greens, after about five months around half of the men have pelagra. Rashes stomach trouble, confusion, even death. By one widely quoted estimate, it killed around 100,000 people across the South. The doctor, Joseph Goldberger, shows pelagra isn't caught like a germ. It comes from something missing in the diet. Later, it's named, niacin, a b-vitamin. In the 1940s, it's added to flour and bread across much of America. With that, better wages and more varied food, pelagra fades. So what is food now? Something we fix at what's missing and a disease disappears. In America that year, a newborn could expect about 55 years, but fixing food only helps if there's enough of it and in India there isn't.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.4, "text": " You wake up in 1915 on a prison farm in Mississippi,", "tokens": [50364, 509, 6634, 493, 294, 1294, 5211, 322, 257, 6168, 5421, 294, 20347, 11, 50574], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1760648312775985, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 0.003008370753377676, "words": [{"word": " You", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.12, "probability": 0.8532892465591431}, {"word": " wake", "start": 0.12, "end": 0.28, "probability": 0.9869250059127808}, {"word": " up", "start": 0.28, "end": 0.48, "probability": 0.9995102882385254}, {"word": " in", "start": 0.48, "end": 0.58, "probability": 0.8842523694038391}, {"word": " 1915", "start": 0.58, "end": 1.38, "probability": 0.9919183552265167}, {"word": " on", "start": 1.38, "end": 2.08, "probability": 0.7796959280967712}, {"word": " a", "start": 2.08, "end": 2.18, "probability": 0.996330201625824}, {"word": " prison", "st